# Aldis Vaunt, the last chorister of Aldruin, three floors under a drowned
# village. He is the SOURCE: the song came up the cistern stairs because he
# has been singing it down here without stopping for eight hundred years.
# He is not hostile. The way past him is a choice, and the kind way is the
# hard roll.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_Cantor

entry rested if flag(TSC_CantorRested)

== start ==
The reliquary is a white stone room with five sockets cut into the altar and one thing in it that is not stone.

He is very old, and the word does not do justice: he is old the way the walls are old.

His hands are folded, and his eyes are open. And he is singing, quietly, in the meter you have been hearing in taverns and on the road and in your sleep since a bard in a distant city put it in your head.

He does not stop immediately when you enter. He gets to the place where the verse stops first.

"You brought a piece of it back."

* "You know what I'm carrying." -> knows
* "Who are you?" -> who
* [Say nothing. Listen to the verse.] -> listen

== listen ==
It is the fifth verse, the one the singers upstairs carry with the words worn smooth and no meaning left in them. Down here, the meaning arrives without the words: a road, a rider, a piece of a crown going east into the dark.

He reaches the place where the verse stops, four bars short of an ending it does not have yet. He begins again. He has begun again a great many times.

* "How long have you been singing?" -> who
* "You know what I'm carrying." -> knows

== who ==
"Aldis Vaunt. Chorister to the crown of Aldruin, which is a small office in a kingdom that no longer exists, so call me what you like."

His head turns, slightly. It is the first thing about him that moves like a living man.

"When the crown broke, the men who carried the pieces out could not write. There was writing, mind. Five roads, cut whole into the stones of the barrow they rode from, and not one of those men could read a stroke of it. A record no rider could carry and no living man could open. So they left the verses with the only man who could hold five of them in his head, and they left him a piece of the crown to hold him together while he held them, and they said: sing it until we come back."

"They did not come back. I have been very punctual about the rest of it."

* "Nobody came for eight hundred years." -> knows
* "There was a beggar who came down here." -> beggar
    if flag(TSC_MetBard)

== beggar ==
Something behind the old face changes, and it takes a moment to recognize it as grief.

"He sat where you are standing for eleven days. He could not read and he could not carry a tune, and he learned the fifth verse anyway, because I sang it and there was nobody else in the world to sing it to."

"Then he went up the stairs with it. I had not managed that in eight hundred years." A pause. "I hoped he lived."

* "He didn't. But the song did. A bard has it now, and she sings it in a full room every night." -> beggar_song
* "He died on the stairs. I'm sorry." -> beggar_song

== beggar_song ==
He is quiet for a long moment.

"Then it is out." Something in his shoulders lets go, eight centuries late. "Then it is out, and it is being sung, and it is not only mine."

* "It isn't. Which means your watch is over." -> rest_offer
* "Tell me about the shard first." -> knows

== knows ==
"A piece of the crown, carried in a bag by someone who does not know what it does yet." He does not sound impressed; he sounds tired. "It preserves. It keeps whatever is nearest to it exactly as it is, for as long as it is near. Stone. Air."

He looks down at his own folded hands.

"Men."

* "It's been keeping you alive." -> keeping
* "Then I'll take it and go." -> take_demand

== keeping ==
"It has been keeping me *here*. Those are different words and the difference is important." His voice does not rise; it has not risen in a long time. "I have wanted to stop since the third century. I cannot stop while it is on the altar, and I cannot take it off the altar, because I was told to keep it, and that instruction is the only part of me that still works properly."

He almost smiles. It is a terrible thing to watch.

"You, however, were told nothing."

* "Then let me end this properly." -> rest_offer
* "I'm sorry." -> rest_offer

== rest_offer ==
"Say the thing that is true," he says, "and say it in the right order, and I will believe it, and then I will be able to stop. That is how it has always worked with me. I am a chorister. I go where the words go."

He waits. It is, you understand, an examination.

* "Do you know where the other shards are?" -> other_shards
* "The crown of Aldruin is being gathered again. Two pieces are in this room. Your watch ended the moment the road took it up." -> rested_scene
    check Lore 9 -> rested_scene | rest_fumble
* "The song is above ground, in a full tavern, in a living woman's voice. It doesn't need a cellar anymore. It doesn't need you." -> rested_scene
    if flag(TSC_MetBard)
    check Persuasion 9 -> rested_scene | rest_fumble
* "I don't need your permission to take it." -> take_demand
* [Leave him to it.] -> end

== other_shards ==

"No. I only knew about this one. And now, the one you have too. You ask me because you want to find all of them, but that is exactly what the shatterers were trying to prevent."

He continues speaking in a low voice.

"I would advise that you forget about your quest; it will not end well for you."

* "Thanks for the advice." -> rest_offer

== rest_fumble ==
The words come out in the wrong order, or the wrong meter, or simply without the weight the room requires. He listens to all of it. He is unfailingly polite.

"No," he says gently. "That is nearly it. Nearly is the same as no, in this work."

He draws breath and begins the verse again, and the room settles back into the shape it has held for eight hundred years.

* "Then I'll take it and be done." -> take_demand
* [Leave him to it.] -> end

== rested_scene ==
He listens the whole way through without moving. Then he nods once. Something has finally balanced.

"Yes. That is right of it."

He stands, and it is not the standing of an old man. It is a chorister taking position. He sings the fifth verse from the top, at full voice, and the sound goes up through three floors of catacomb and a drowned village and out into the world it was written for.

He stops where the verse stops, on the phrase that does not resolve, and holds that note longer than any breath should hold it. When it ends, the vestments settle to the floor, and the eight hundred years arrive all at once, and there is nothing left standing to arrive at.

The shard he kept lies where he fell. The chest with the old king's sword stands unguarded. The altar is quiet.

* [Take up what he kept.] -> end
    do flag(TSC_CantorRested); signal(TSC_Act2_Cellars, TSC_CantorRested); grant_xp(150); affinity(+5)

== take_demand ==
"No," he says, with real regret. "I am sorry. I was told to keep it."

He rises, and the blade he draws is not for show. He has kept this post, alone, for a very long time, and the shard rides in his coat where his heart used to do the work.

"I would have preferred the other way."

* [Fight him for it.] -> end
    do flag(TSC_CantorFought); npc_hostile()
* [Step back.] -> end

== rested ==
Gray vestments on white stone, and the room finally quiet.

* [Go.] -> end
